  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Querer vs Gustar \u2014 Wanting vs Liking",
      "content": "QUERER (to want) works like a normal verb:\n- Yo quiero... (I want...)\n- T\u00fa quieres... (You want...)\n- \u00c9l quiere... (He wants...)\n\nGUSTAR (to like) works differently \u2014 it means 'to be pleasing to':\n- Me gusta el caf\u00e9 (Coffee is pleasing to me = I like coffee)\n- Me gustan los caf\u00e9s (Coffees are pleasing to me = I like coffees)\n- Te gusta la comida (Food is pleasing to you)\n- Le gusta el vino (Wine is pleasing to him/her)\n\nPLURAL RULE: 'gusta' (singular) vs 'gustan' (plural)\n- Me gusta el t\u00e9 (I like tea \u2014 singular)\n- Me gustan los t\u00e9s (I like teas \u2014 plural)",
      "examples": [
        "Quiero un caf\u00e9, por favor. (I want a coffee, please.)\nMe gusta el chocolate. (I like chocolate.)\nMe gustan las galletas. (I like cookies.)",
        "\u00bfTe gusta la sopa? (Do you like soup?)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Ser vs Estar in Restaurants",
      "content": "SER for permanent qualities of food:\n- El caf\u00e9 es bueno. (Coffee is good \u2014 generally)\n- La paella es deliciosa. (Paella is delicious \u2014 generally)\n\nESTAR for current state of food:\n- La comida est\u00e1 caliente. (The food is hot \u2014 right now)\n- La sopa est\u00e1 fr\u00eda. (The soup is cold \u2014 right now)\n- El plato est\u00e1 sucio. (The plate is dirty \u2014 right now)\n\nCommon restaurant states:\n- \u00bfEst\u00e1 libre esta mesa? (Is this table free?)\n- La comida est\u00e1 lista. (The food is ready.)\n- Est\u00e1 delicioso. (It's delicious \u2014 how it tastes right now).",
      "examples": [
        "La carne es buena pero est\u00e1 demasiado salada. (The meat is good but it's too salty right now.)\n\u00bfEst\u00e1 libre la mesa de la ventana? (Is the window table free?)",
        "El restaurante es grande y est\u00e1 limpio. (The restaurant is big and it's clean.)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "pronunciation": [
    {
      "title": "The C before E/I \u2014 Soft C Sound",
      "content": "In Spanish, 'c' before 'e' or 'i' sounds like the English 'th' (Spain) or 's' (Latin America).\n\nSpain: c + e/i = 'th' sound\n- cinco = SIN-koh (Spain) / SIN-koh (LatAm)\n- ciudad = thee-DAHd (Spain) / see-DAHd (LatAm)\n- centro = SEN-tro (Spain) / SEN-tro (LatAm)\n\nC before a/o/u = hard 'k' sound:\n- casa = KAHS-ah\n- color = KOL-or\n- cocina = ko-SEE-nah\n\nThis is different from English where 'c' can sound like 's' or 'k' unpredictably.",
      "examples": [
        "cerveza (sehr-VEH-sah / therr-VEH-thah)\ncaf\u00e9 (kah-FEH)\ncocina (ko-SEE-nah)\nchocolate (choh-ko-LAH-teh)"
      ]
    }
  ],
